<h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
<h2>Yoreh Day'ah II &nbsp;·&nbsp; Laws of <i>Milah</i> [Circumcision] &nbsp;·&nbsp; Halacha 1</h2>

<div class="lead-verse">
  <i>Milah</i> [circumcision] performed at its proper time — that is, on the eighth day — overrides the Sabbath.
</div>

<p>For it is found in the words of our Rebbe, in the discourse <em>"A Prayer of Chavakuk"</em> [Likutay Moharan I, <span class="source">§19</span>], that <span class="key">milah</span> [circumcision] is an aspect of the perfection of <span class="key">lashon hakodesh</span> [the holy tongue] through <span class="key">lashon targum</span> [the Aramaic translation tongue] — which [the <i>targum</i>] is the aspect of <span class="key">tardemah</span> [deep sleep / dormancy], meaning: through the subduing of the evil of the seventy languages — which is the aspect of the <span class="key">orlah</span> [foreskin / the covering] that is cut — through this the evil within the targum is subdued, and the good within it ascends to the holy tongue.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key">chituch u'fri'ah</span> [the cutting and the uncovering]: namely, the cutting of the <i>orlah</i> is the aspect of cutting away and expelling the collective evil of the seventy nations, for the <i>orlah</i> is the aspect of the three impure <span class="key">kelipos</span> [husks / shells of impurity], which are the aspect of the seventy languages. And the <i>fri'ah</i> [the uncovering of the corona] is the aspect of <span class="key">nogah</span> [the intermediate luminous husk], which is the aspect of <i>targum</i>, the aspect of <i>tardemah</i> that ascends to holiness through the subduing of the <i>orlah</i> as mentioned above — for through the <i>fri'ah</i>, the evil within the <i>tardemah</i> is extracted and subdued and broken, and the good within it ascends to holiness. And therefore, one who circumcises but does not perform <i>fri'ah</i> is as though he did not circumcise at all, for the essence of the <i>milah</i> is for the sake of the <i>fri'ah</i>, through which the holy tongue is made complete, as stated above.</p>

<p>And therefore the <i>milah</i> is performed on the eighth day of his birth — so that the seven days may pass over him with the <i>orlah</i> attached to him, and afterward it is subdued. And this is the aspect of the perfection of the holy tongue through <i>targum</i>. For I heard from our Rebbe [of blessed memory] that the perfection of the holy tongue through <i>targum</i> is the aspect of <i>milah</i>. For the heretics ask: if the Holy One Blessed is He desires the <i>milah</i>, why was man not created already circumcised? — as is brought in the Midrash. But according to what is stated there in the above-mentioned discourse, that the holy tongue which comes from Above — even though it comes from Above — nevertheless cannot be made perfect except through the <i>targum</i>, meaning: through those below subduing the evil within the <i>targum</i> and elevating the good [therein] to the holy tongue — through this specifically is the holy tongue made perfect. Similarly this is the aspect of <i>milah</i>, meaning that it is specifically necessary to perfect the holiness from below. See there carefully and understand.</p>

<p>And to explain the matter somewhat: for it is known that the essence of the perfection of holiness is through <span class="key">bechirah</span> [free will / free choice], for this is the essence of His blessed will — that we should have free will specifically, meaning that the <span class="key">sitra achra</span> [the other side] should have a foothold to entice and to turn [one] away from the good path. And through our subjugating it and subduing the evil — through this specifically is the holiness made perfect. And this is the essence of the perfection of holiness. And this is the aspect of the perfection of the holy tongue through <i>targum</i>, for the <i>targum</i> is the aspect of <i>bechirah</i>, which is the intermediary between good and evil. And then the intermediate [state] between good and evil — which is the aspect of <i>targum</i>, the aspect of <i>bechirah</i> — is automatically established, wherein one has the power to choose this [the good] or the opposite, G-d forbid. And through subjugating the <i>sitra achra</i> and subduing the evil — through this the evil within the aspect of <i>bechirah</i> — that is, the evil within the <i>targum</i> — is automatically subdued. And then the good ascends to holiness. And this is the aspect of the perfection of the holy tongue through <i>targum</i> — as already explained above in the Laws of Mixtures [<span class="source">Halacha 1</span>].</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <i>milah</i> — that it is for this reason that Hashem blessed be He created man with an <i>orlah</i>: so that there would be in him the hold of the <i>orlah</i>, which is the collective evil, in order that he would have <i>bechirah</i>. For it is only for this reason that a person comes into the world — to be tested by this trial of desire [the <i>orlah</i>], for it is the primary scale of [moral] balance. And afterward it is cut and subdued, and then the holiness is made perfect — for this is the essence of the perfection of holiness: the aspect of the perfection of the holy tongue through <i>targum</i> as mentioned, that is, through <i>bechirah</i> as mentioned. And therefore the [<i>orlah</i>] remains attached to him all seven days, for the seven days are the aspect of the collective of seventy years — as is brought in the sacred books, that during these seventy years the <i>sitra achra</i> has a foothold, and within them the aspect of <span class="key">sheinah</span> [sleep], the aspect of <i>tardemah</i>, grows powerful — as it is written: <em>"The days of our years, in them are seventy years… You carry them away as a flood, they are as a sleep"</em> [<span class="source">Psalms 90:10, 5</span>]. And one must subdue the sleep — that is, subdue the evil and elevate the good, as mentioned.</p>

<p>And this is what the hold of the <i>orlah</i> for seven days alludes to: the empowerment of the <i>tardemah</i> throughout all seven days, which are inclusive of seventy years in which the power of <i>bechirah</i> resides. And afterward it is cut and subdued — which is the aspect of the perfection of holiness through <i>targum</i> as mentioned.</p>

<p>And therefore <i>milah</i> overrides the Sabbath. For the essence of the sanctity of the Sabbath is that then the <i>tardemah</i> — in the aspect of <i>nogah</i> — ascends to holiness, and the collective evil, which are the three <i>kelipos</i>, is entirely subdued at that time — as is explained there in the abovementioned Torah teaching regarding the washing with hot water on <i>Erev Shabbos</i> [the eve of the Sabbath]; see there. And therefore it is specifically necessary to circumcise him even on the Sabbath, in order to subdue the evil mentioned above and to elevate the <i>targum</i> to holiness — which is the essence of the sanctity of the Sabbath, as mentioned.</p>

<p>And therefore the mitzvah of <i>milah</i> is performed specifically by day, for the essence of the mitzvah of <i>milah</i> is to subdue the <i>tardemah</i> that dominates at night. And by day one can subdue it and nullify it. Amen.</p>

<hr class="divider">

<div class="summary-box">
  <h3>Translator's Summary — Laws of <i>Milah</i>, Halacha 1</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Core principle:</strong> <em>Milah</em> overrides the Sabbath because it is the very foundation of Sabbath holiness.</li>
    <li><strong>Holy tongue &amp; Aramaic translation:</strong> The holy tongue (<em>lashon hakodesh</em>) achieves its perfection specifically through the Aramaic translation (<em>targum</em>). The <em>targum</em> occupies the intermediate zone of free will — neither purely good nor purely evil — and by subduing the evil within it, the good rises to holiness. This parallel is the key to understanding <em>milah</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Cutting and uncovering (<em>chituch u'fri'ah</em>):</strong> The cutting (<em>chituch</em>) removes the entirely evil outer husk (the three impure <em>kelipos</em>, representing the seventy nations). The uncovering (<em>fri'ah</em>) addresses the intermediate husk of <em>nogah</em> (the mixed good-evil aspect — akin to <em>targum</em>), extracting its evil so the good within it can ascend. One who circumcises without <em>fri'ah</em> has not truly circumcised.</li>
    <li><strong>Why man is born with a foreskin:</strong> Man is born with an <em>orlah</em> to provide the foothold for free will. The <em>orlah</em> clings for seven days, representing seventy years of life in which free will operates. On the eighth day it is subdued — perfecting holiness through the exercise of choice.</li>
    <li><strong>Why <em>milah</em> overrides Shabbos:</strong> On Shabbos, the <em>nogah</em> / <em>tardemah</em> ascends to holiness and the three impure <em>kelipos</em> are fully subdued. The act of <em>milah</em> achieves exactly this — it is therefore not a violation of Shabbos but rather the very vehicle of Shabbos holiness.</li>
    <li><strong>Why <em>milah</em> is performed by day:</strong> The <em>tardemah</em> (dormancy / sleep / the evil husks) dominates at night; by day one has the power to subdue and nullify it.</li>
  </ul>
